WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.690
CEASAR MCDOWELL: The people
who are living in cities

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.350
represent a complexity
of the human experience

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.540
that we've never
organized around.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.270
If you have a democracy,
and you need your public

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.480
to be able to be
engaged, you need

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.770
to have a system for doing that.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.560
We actually don't have one
for that kind of public.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.712
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.712 --> 00:00:22.170
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on the podcast,

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.710
we're exploring the world of
civic design and re-imagining

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.680
what public engagement in
demographically complex

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.480
communities might look like.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.750
CEASAR MCDOWELL: There
are no complete solutions.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.060
You have to learn
how to make decisions

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.360
in the midst of ambiguity,
which means, for you internally,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.030
you have to have your own
dialogue with yourself.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.240
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.630
In this episode, I'm talking
with an MIT professor

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.730
who bravely navigates
along the deepest

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.640
rifts in our public
discourse, and who

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.060
works with students and
communities alike to step

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.760
consciously into these spaces.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.580
My guest today is
Professor Ceasar McDowell,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.750
who teaches course 11.312,
Engaging Community.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.210
But before we get
into our conversation,

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.850
we wanted to pause to
remember that so many

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.840
of our OCW team members,
and probably many of you,

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.300
have lost people that they
love over this past year.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.440
And so we're dedicating this
last episode of Season 2

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.380
to all of our loved ones,
and to all of yours,

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.690
and to all of those who
carry their light forward.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.360
Professor McDowell
approaches his work

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.310
with community engagement
like it's an art form.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:42.150
In fact, he's built an
entire design framework

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.960
for practitioners to shape and
facilitate these engagements.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.030
But before we get
into all of that,

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.650
we need to understand
the main problem

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:53.100
his work seeks to address,
his why, if you will.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.600
CEASAR MCDOWELL:
Things are actually

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.830
changing in this country.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.200
Our populations are changing.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.030
And right now, in
cities, I think

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.600
we're just seeing that more
pronounced than anywhere else.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.100
Right?

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.950
That the people who
are living in cities

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:12.330
just happen to represent a
complexity of human experience

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.790
that we've never
organized around.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:18.270
And if you have a democracy,
and you need your public

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.490
to be able to be
engaged, you need

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.740
to have a system for doing that.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.561
We actually don't have one
for that kind of public.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.850
SARAH HANSEN: A lot of Professor
McDowell's course and work

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.740
center around the
idea of democracy.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.640
But in this episode, when
we talk about democracy,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.580
we're not talking about
elections and voting.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.860
When Professor McDowell
speaks of democracy,

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.800
he's talking about the
engagement, the communities,

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.580
the conversations, the processes
that we have as a public

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.920
to come together
and make decisions.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.290
And a lot of these processes
that we traditionally

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.812
consider democracy in action
are a little outdated.

00:02:57.812 --> 00:02:59.270
CEASAR MCDOWELL:
I'll tell you what

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:00.937
I mean by we don't
have a democracy that

00:03:00.937 --> 00:03:02.180
works for who we are.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.260
I'll give you an example of
how we hold on to something

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.570
as a way of thinking about
democracy that does not

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.730
serve us well right now.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.060
You can go back to the
founding of the country.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.712
And you look at the
original colonies.

00:03:17.712 --> 00:03:19.170
They weren't the
original colonies,

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.000
the people came and
occupied the land

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.280
and created these opportunities
for themselves here.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.100
And if you look at those,
they created this thing

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.900
that we hold as a
myth today, which

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.750
is this thing of the New
England town meeting, town hall.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.620
But if we're really serious
about looking at that,

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.275
there were some things that
were very true about that.

00:03:39.275 --> 00:03:44.150
It was mostly open to men
who were property owners.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.730
Most of these people who
were men and property owners

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:50.640
lived in enclaves that
shared a religious belief.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.140
OK?

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.610
So you take that as a core, and
you then build your processes

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:00.170
around that core that allow
you to come to decisions.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.550
Over time, what we've
been doing is saying,

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.330
well, we don't want
to be that exclusive.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.760
We want to let a
few more people in.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.957
So we modify that
a little bit more.

00:04:11.957 --> 00:04:13.790
And over time, that's
what we've been doing.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.579
We've been modifying it
more, modifying it more.

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:19.740
And then you get to something
like the presidential debates.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.118
And what do we have?

00:04:21.118 --> 00:04:22.910
What do we say is the
way we let the public

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:24.350
into this conversation?

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:30.020
These televised town halls, they
have no reality or connection

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.180
to the complexity of who we are.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:37.250
But we hold on to
them as our evidence

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:42.800
that we are doing and engaging
the public around a dialogue.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.050
So this is what I mean
is we don't have it.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:48.380
We actually don't have a
functioning infrastructure

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.560
for democracy, given the
complexity of who we are.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.810
So that's what I mean by that.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:55.190
It's not just about voting.

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:58.070
It's the infrastructure
that allows

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.670
people to do the work that
the public has to do in order

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.920
for democracy to function.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.420
SARAH HANSEN: The
new infrastructure

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.130
Professor McDowell
is talking about

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:11.580
here involves a shift away
from the more traditional forms

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.280
of civic engagement
and participation.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.230
Professor McDowell's
way involves

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.720
looking at a given situation
from multiple perspectives,

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.290
and then spending time to
reflect on our own values

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.022
and the needs of our community.

00:05:24.022 --> 00:05:25.730
CEASAR MCDOWELL: At
one point in my life,

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:29.410
I lived in Alaska way up
above the Arctic Circle.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.560
I was associate superintendent
of a new school system there

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.960
that was the size of
the State of Indiana.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.030
It had 100 villages,
5,000 people, no roads.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:38.530
OK?

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:42.710
So that's the context,
predominantly Inupiat.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:46.270
And there was this one
instance in the village where

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:51.070
we had a teacher who was
actually not performing

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.120
very well in the school.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.560
And we were really concerned.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.360
The principal was concerned.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:56.800
We were concerned.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.420
And the teacher was Anglo in
this predominantly Inupiat

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:02.800
village.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.770
And we had decided to remove
the teacher, because this

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.990
was the best thing for the kids,
just wasn't performing well.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.020
And there was a
meeting in the village,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.040
where we talked about that.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.920
And there was a
lot of resistance.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:19.960
And so the
superintendent asked me

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.290
to go and try to find
out what's going on.

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:26.353
And so I went, in these little
villages you could do it.

00:06:26.353 --> 00:06:27.520
I talked to almost everyone.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.300
[LAUGHS]

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.200
I talked to a lot of
people in the village.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.830
And here's what was happening.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:37.390
The school was saying, this
person is not a good teacher.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.450
And people were saying, this
person is a good teacher.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.910
And we couldn't reconcile.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:48.280
But what we learned, when we
started to talk to people,

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:52.030
is that we were evaluating the
teacher in very different ways.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:55.972
Inside the school, the
good was really applied--

00:06:55.972 --> 00:06:57.430
For the school
system, the good was

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:01.120
applied to how well
is this person taking

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.670
care of the educational
needs of the children.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:11.830
For some of the people in the
community, teacher was a role.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:14.890
Good was the value
to the community.

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:17.980
She was good for the community
because of other functions

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.940
she played in the community.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:23.800
And so we had this totally
different use of language.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.033
And they knew that if she
wasn't there as a teacher,

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:26.950
she wouldn't be there.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:28.330
Because she'd have to leave.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:33.010
And so other values to the
community would be lost.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:37.090
That kind of perspective
taking, I think, is so required.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:41.030
And as we find ways to
bring people together,

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:44.050
we have to start to
construct mechanisms

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.090
that allow people to do
that kind of reflection

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:48.760
and that kind of perspective
taking in relationship

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.902
to the other.

00:07:50.902 --> 00:07:53.110
SARAH HANSEN: One of the
challenges of getting people

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.630
to practice tuning into
different perspectives

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:57.190
is a physical one.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:58.900
In our current
political climate,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.300
how do we even get people
with different perspectives

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.520
in the same room?

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.585
CEASAR MCDOWELL: So
we start this campaign

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:07.390
where we're testing out now.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:09.577
It's called America's
Path Forward.

00:08:09.577 --> 00:08:11.410
In the campaign, we're
actually reaching out

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:12.410
to people in the public.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:16.910
And we're asking them to do one
thing, actually, two things.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.360
One is to donate
the question they

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.040
have about the future of
America and the experience

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.910
of their life that led
them to their question.

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.040
And then what we do
is we bring people

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:33.820
who have similar questions,
and sometimes not,

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.460
into small conversations.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.710
In those conversations,
all people

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:44.230
are doing is sharing a question,
sharing their experience.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.720
And what people
find is, one, when

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.370
they go into these
rooms with people

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:50.350
who have similar
questions, they're

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.386
surprised at who's in the room.

00:08:52.386 --> 00:08:56.277
Because the thing that
they hold as what's theirs,

00:08:56.277 --> 00:08:58.360
they're realizing that's
a different set of people

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.050
who actually hold this too.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:02.270
And then when they have
different experiences,

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.480
they're beginning
to realize, oh,

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.870
this question manifests itself
in totally different ways.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:09.370
Right?

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:12.100
It's the same question,
but it's living out

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:13.960
differently in our society.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:15.820
And for me, this
is a way of helping

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:18.820
the public build its own kind
of reflection and connection

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:19.960
to others.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:22.450
And what we try to
get people to do

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:23.920
at the end of
these conversations

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:27.040
is to say, if you could
reframe this question so

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:31.750
that if it was addressed,
it would more likely attend

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.360
to the different ways
you are experiencing,

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.520
how would you
reframe the question?

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:39.010
Just getting people to
start and think that way,

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:42.490
holding the public as
curious intellectual beings,

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:46.870
and encouraging them in that,
to say, yes, this is hard work.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.638
And yes, you can do it.

00:09:49.638 --> 00:09:51.930
SARAH HANSEN: Professor
McDowell has worked with a team

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:55.530
to create a tool for having
better public conversations.

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:58.290
The tool is called the
Civic Design Framework.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:00.030
I was curious about
how the design

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:02.863
element fits into this kind
of community engagement work.

00:10:02.863 --> 00:10:03.780
CEASAR MCDOWELL: Yeah.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.630
So when I think about community
engagement and design,

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:08.130
the reason I put
those two together

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:11.880
is to talk about the
purposefulness of how we go out

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.580
in the world and create
engagement processes.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:18.180
And that they actually are
designed and can be designed.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.050
The main thing I'm trying to get
people to pay attention to is,

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:25.320
when you're putting together
an engagement process,

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.260
you have the ability
to construct it

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.953
for certain kinds of
outcomes and processes

00:10:30.953 --> 00:10:33.120
that you want to have happen
and the connections you

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:33.953
want to have happen.

00:10:33.953 --> 00:10:36.430
And therefore, that's
a design challenge.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:38.430
Given the population you
work with and the issue

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:39.930
you're going out
to the public with,

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:43.410
how then do you construct that?

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:46.500
The sets of things that can
happen, so that they actually

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:52.140
allow you to bring people, not
just closer together, but also,

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.210
what I like to say is
help people, the public,

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:57.758
build its muscle for democracy.

00:10:57.758 --> 00:11:00.050
SARAH HANSEN: The Civic Design
Framework gives us a way

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:02.420
into these conversations
that are central to having

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:04.580
a truly functional democracy.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.470
Professor McDowell
teaches the framework

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:10.490
to students in 11.312,
Engaging Community.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:13.490
CEASAR MCDOWELL: We, really,
use the Civic Design Framework

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:17.360
as a tool for organizing
thinking and projects.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.790
And the framework really
has two components to it.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:23.000
It's based on the
notion that part

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.760
of the issue of
bringing the public

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:29.000
into any engagement
process is we're not

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:33.103
clear about what conversation
we're inviting them into.

00:11:33.103 --> 00:11:35.270
Our problem with our public
discourse and engagement

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.940
process is we often invite
people into one conversation.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:39.560
And then when they
get in the room,

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.550
we set them up for another.

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.840
So we will say to them,
tell them, imagine

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:46.940
a new Sarasota, Florida.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:51.410
And you go into the room, and
you have three choices to make.

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.690
So basically, you've
set people up.

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:58.340
And no wonder people
are really dissatisfied.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.650
The other thing why
it's important to know

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:02.150
which kind of
conversation you have,

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:05.240
is that they each require
something different.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:07.700
If you want to be in a framing
conversation with someone,

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.200
that's totally different.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:12.860
You're using really different
cognitive structures

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.615
than if you're in
the ideation process.

00:12:15.615 --> 00:12:17.240
Because in the ideation
process, you're

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:19.920
trying to ignite imagination.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.470
In a framing
conversation, you're

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.840
really trying to
deeply understand

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.100
experience and what's
happening in the world.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:27.772
So those are very different.

00:12:27.772 --> 00:12:29.855
And so if you don't know
what kind of conversation

00:12:29.855 --> 00:12:31.670
you want to have
in public, you're

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:35.200
not going to design
it the right way.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.700
SARAH HANSEN: What's
really exciting

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:39.070
about Professor
McDowell's class is

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:41.740
that he doesn't just teach the
theory of this Civic Design

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:42.580
Framework.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:45.790
Students are also invited to
bring forward actual cases

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:47.770
from the communities with
which they're already

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:50.140
engaging, where they
can implement the design

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:52.700
framework in real life.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:54.560
CEASAR MCDOWELL: I
always try to generate

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:57.470
this opportunity
for students to say,

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:59.660
here are the things
we want to work on.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.840
And then to negotiate among
themselves which ones they

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:04.970
really want to move forward on.

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:08.570
We did one in Mexico, and that
one, it's actually interesting.

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:11.270
It was a former alum
of our department

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:16.220
who had just stepped into this
major role in a new government

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:18.660
in a town in Mexico.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:23.400
And they had got there through
a popular, all grassroot

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:24.030
campaign.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.447
And now they wanted
to figure out,

00:13:25.447 --> 00:13:27.855
how do we carry that
into a structure,

00:13:27.855 --> 00:13:29.850
into governance,
that actually allows

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:31.710
to keep the public involved?

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:33.405
And so the class was
really helping them

00:13:33.405 --> 00:13:34.300
through that design.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:38.867
So it's always that some
problem, some place,

00:13:38.867 --> 00:13:40.950
where people are trying
to figure out how we broad

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.938
and diversify how they're
engaging with the public.

00:13:43.938 --> 00:13:45.945
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:45.945 --> 00:13:48.070
SARAH HANSEN: One of the
realities of collaborating

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:50.230
with real people
in real communities

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:52.450
is that practitioners
often find themselves

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:55.540
in situations where
solutions are complicated

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.850
and, sometimes, not complete.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:00.700
Professor McDowell offered
some important words of wisdom

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.015
for practitioners
new to field work.

00:14:03.015 --> 00:14:05.140
CEASAR MCDOWELL: I think
it's not just this course,

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:07.770
but in any real
practice-based course,

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:09.920
that puts you out
into the field.

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.530
There are a couple of things
that you have to contend with.

00:14:12.530 --> 00:14:15.320
One, there are no
complete solutions.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.600
You have to learn
how to make decisions

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:22.110
in the midst of ambiguity.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:24.470
You just, you have
to do it, which

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:26.540
means, for you
internally, you have

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:32.030
to have your own
dialogue with yourself

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.490
to understand how you're
holding onto the things

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:35.630
that you believe.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:39.390
Even if you're not able
to really achieve them,

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.050
within the midst of any one
project or any one initiative,

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:45.770
but you're still not
letting it get to you.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:47.900
And one of the ways
we talk about this

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:50.810
is I make a real
distinction in our class

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:53.520
around how we talk about things.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:56.420
So for example, in
our society, we're

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:57.935
really good at
what I would say is

00:14:57.935 --> 00:15:01.250
this kind of language of
evaluation, how well something

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:01.850
is or not.

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:04.190
And we're also good at
language of critique which

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:05.760
is different than evaluation.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:09.870
And we're really good at
this language of possibility.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:11.720
We're looking into the future.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.090
What we're not really
good at is talking

00:15:14.090 --> 00:15:18.170
about the reality, what I call
the language of transition.

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:22.010
And the reality is we
have things we want to do.

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:24.800
And as we're trying to do
those things to change systems

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.220
we want to be different, we
have to support the systems

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.860
that we're trying to change.

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:32.180
And so we live in
this in-between space,

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:33.615
this space called Nepantla.

00:15:33.615 --> 00:15:34.490
That's where we live.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:36.427
And in that space,
we have to learn

00:15:36.427 --> 00:15:37.510
how to talk to each other.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:39.177
And this is why I
always use these terms

00:15:39.177 --> 00:15:40.820
like grace and stuff like that.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.895
Because of the recognition
that that's where you are.

00:15:43.895 --> 00:15:46.700
So in my class, I
try to support folks

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:51.170
of being able to talk about
that, about what they're living

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:53.240
with in the middle,
what they're having

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:56.218
to hold onto that they don't
necessarily feel good about.

00:15:56.218 --> 00:15:58.010
But they know they have
to do it as they're

00:15:58.010 --> 00:15:59.260
trying to do this other thing.

00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:02.300
And to support them through
that kind of conversation.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.120
This is a reflection
of the transition

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.290
you're living in, and just
a recognition of that,

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:11.090
and honoring it, and
letting it be OK.

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:16.240
It's almost being able to
have a dialogue of forgiveness

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:16.880
with yourself.

00:16:19.273 --> 00:16:21.690
SARAH HANSEN: I wanted to know
what these engagements look

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:23.500
like in practice.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.440
So Professor McDowell
shared with me

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.240
one example that took place in
my very own city of Cambridge,

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:29.760
Massachusetts.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.100
The dialogue was around
domestic violence

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.830
and how the city could
better address the issue.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:36.690
And the engagement was simple.

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:39.840
Professor McDowell and his team
asked residents of Cambridge

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:43.610
to donate a question they
had about domestic violence.

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:45.740
CEASAR MCDOWELL: Think
about domestic violence.

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:48.980
That is not a
public conversation.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:51.740
That's not a thing
that people talk about.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:54.950
Because we have a
structure of all

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:56.800
you have to do is
donate a question,

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:59.800
a group of young people from
Cambridge Rindge and Latin

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:02.590
set up a booth at
Harvard Square.

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:06.069
They put up a board,
and they made cookies.

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:10.089
And they just basically had
a cookie for your question.

00:17:10.089 --> 00:17:12.520
And people would
write their question

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:15.810
about domestic violence and put
it on board and get a cookie.

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:18.970
And then people start
having conversations

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:24.239
in public about what's on the
board and what they're hearing.

00:17:24.239 --> 00:17:26.364
So you're taking something
that we don't talk about

00:17:26.364 --> 00:17:27.460
and moving forward.

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:28.990
At the same time,
what we did is we

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:31.120
took all the questions
that came in,

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:33.950
printed them out very large.

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:37.570
And we had a space in
Cambridge that we could use,

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:41.900
where we put all 2,000-something
questions up on the wall.

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:43.490
And we created a
public event where

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:46.790
people would come in,
read the questions,

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:48.320
and sit down to
have conversations

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.087
with each other about what
they were seeing and hearing.

00:17:51.087 --> 00:17:52.670
And then we had
people from the public

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:54.060
come in and say,
let's figure out,

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:56.060
can we organize these
questions in certain kinds

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:57.423
of themes and issues?

00:17:57.423 --> 00:17:59.090
And then we had
conversation with people

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:01.310
about themes and issues.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:05.840
So all of this is taking
something we don't talk about

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:08.870
and creating an opportunity
for people to do it.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:13.380
Because you're, in some sense,
recognizing that everyone

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.080
has something to say about this.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:18.238
But if you do it
in the right way,

00:18:18.238 --> 00:18:20.280
you don't try to tell them
what they have to say,

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:22.110
or how they have
to say it, you just

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:26.040
create an opportunity
that equalizes voices.

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:26.685
People step up.

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:32.440
SARAH HANSEN: This idea about
equalizing voices and bringing

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.450
everyone to the
table is incredibly

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:36.940
important in Professor
McDowell's work.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:39.490
One specific aspect of
his design framework

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:41.710
speaks directly to this idea.

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:43.750
It's built on the
notion of equity.

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:47.095
And it's a concept he calls
designing for the margins.

00:18:47.095 --> 00:18:48.470
CEASAR MCDOWELL:
The reason I use

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.170
this concept of
design for the margins

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:54.280
is our default in
everything we do in design

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:58.420
is designed for the middle,
where most people are.

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:00.310
And we do that because
we want to scale.

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:01.720
We want things to grow fast.

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:04.400
We want to make sure we're
taking care of a lot of people.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:06.550
But the thing about
when you design

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:08.020
for those that
are in the middle,

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:10.450
you're mostly
designing for people

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:14.860
who, more or less, can operate
within systems that are there.

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:17.710
And then what that ends up
doing is pushing people margins

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:20.101
further and further out.

00:19:20.101 --> 00:19:23.060
And as other things
in our systems fail,

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:26.115
we end up putting more and
more people into that margin.

00:19:26.115 --> 00:19:28.490
And by the margins,
I mean those who

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:31.440
are living with the
failures of the society.

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:35.110
And so if we're going
to attend to that,

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:37.120
and actually bring
their voices in,

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:41.980
we have to first design
opportunities and engagement

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:44.650
processes that work
for them first.

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:47.500
Because those in the middle
will find their way into that.

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.260
Because they have the
resources, the opportunity.

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:52.430
They know how to
negotiate the system.

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:55.480
The other thing when you
design for the margins you do

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:58.180
is, because they're
clearly living

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.230
with the failures
of the systems,

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:03.400
you're able to design around
the things that would normally

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:05.310
keep people out.

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:10.852
So it's a purposeful thing that
actually benefits more people.

00:20:10.852 --> 00:20:12.810
If you actually do that--
and we have instances

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:17.380
of that in everyday life that
shows that, some physical ones,

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.780
like the curb cut.

00:20:19.780 --> 00:20:21.970
The curb cut was a
thing that came up,

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:26.080
very particular movement from
the disabilities community

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.050
decades ago to
create some access,

00:20:29.050 --> 00:20:30.773
and a lot of fights
to get it done,

00:20:30.773 --> 00:20:32.190
but the interesting
thing about is

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:34.750
is you look at it now and say,
well, who benefits from that?

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:37.900
That's a design for a very
small population of the country.

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:39.390
Who benefits from it?

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:41.020
Who uses it?

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:44.080
Well, everybody that rides
a bicycle, pushes a trailer,

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:45.492
carries a grocery cart.

00:20:45.492 --> 00:20:46.450
SARAH HANSEN: Stroller.

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:47.408
CEASAR MCDOWELL: Right?

00:20:47.408 --> 00:20:49.253
Strollers, it's
all there, right?

00:20:49.253 --> 00:20:50.920
But that's not what
it was designed for.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:53.920
It was designed for
the margin, but yet,

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:57.310
all these central activities
that people are involved in

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:59.950
and what I would
call the middle,

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:02.397
are able to benefit
from that too.

00:21:02.397 --> 00:21:04.480
The interesting thing about
it, and why I actually

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.630
like to use an example,
is what have you

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:12.820
noticed about changes in
curb cuts in the last decade?

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:15.250
They have bumps on them now.

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:16.450
They have texture to them.

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:21.970
And they have texture to
them, because it helps people

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:26.320
without sight to know if
they're coming to a corner.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:28.390
So the thing about
designing for the margins,

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:31.450
it's not the thing
you've achieve.

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:33.700
It's a thing that you're
always working at.

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:38.230
Because the margins
will keep emerging.

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:40.220
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
anything like me,

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:43.240
you may be thinking to
yourself that this is exactly

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:45.250
the kind of work
that our country

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:48.490
and, really, our planet
could use more of, better,

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:52.240
more open, more connected ways
to engage groups of people

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:55.330
around complex issues, and
a design framework that

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:58.190
allows us to keep
working at those issues.

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:00.670
Part of what I love about
Professor McDowell's work

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:03.850
is that it's grounded in doing
the hard work of community

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:06.253
in community together.

00:22:06.253 --> 00:22:08.420
CEASAR MCDOWELL: The way
that our systems are set up

00:22:08.420 --> 00:22:12.320
right now, they really are
designed to keep people apart,

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:14.300
to disconnect people
from each other,

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:15.800
or groups from each other.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:18.410
And they draw these
false dichotomies.

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:21.200
But in reality, people live
with a much more complex set

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:22.550
of things.

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:25.580
So for example, you take the
movement for Black lives.

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:29.480
And you take the way that,
in a very powerful way, using

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:31.880
the notions of defund the
police, and [? some other ?]

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:35.630
views, and others to
change what policing is.

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:40.220
Because they have seen that
evidence over and over again

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:46.170
of the devaluing of Black lives
within the midst of the police.

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:50.220
However, even with that same
experience, knowledge base,

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:52.890
you can go into the
Black community,

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.380
and people have very
different feelings

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:58.650
about do we defund
the police or not?

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:02.310
The trading off of their
I know there's a risk,

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:04.530
but that risk is in
here, and it's out there,

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:06.130
and I don't know
how to balance it.

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:08.630
I don't know what
to do about that.

00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:12.360
When we put things
in an either or, then

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:16.680
we really lose the opportunity
to really connect people,

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:18.570
to create some
opportunity for people

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:23.430
to acknowledge what's
the complexity out there.

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:25.890
And how do you go through
that in a way that

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:27.540
doesn't leave people behind?

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.470
Or leave them to the
side, I should say.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:32.490
You can use policing as some.

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:35.290
You could use what's happening
with vaccinations as another.

00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:37.740
There's a lot of things
that are just structured.

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:40.740
And the way we talk
about them in the public

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:43.620
doesn't recognize one thing
that we know is really true.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:45.590
Our values kind of
lie on a continuum.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:49.710
And what we're always doing is
trading off one for the other.

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:51.990
We're going back and forth.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:53.765
People looking at
things, and they're

00:23:53.765 --> 00:23:56.520
looking at that value
versus another value,

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:58.590
versus another value.

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:00.850
And why that's important
for public conversations

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:05.090
is, if we can step away from
saying, your value's this

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:07.170
and mine is that, and
you can get people

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:09.686
in the space to realize, oh,
this is how you're trading off

00:24:09.686 --> 00:24:10.530
this set of values.

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.660
And this is how I'm
trading them off.

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:14.730
Then what you realize is
that people are holding

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:17.830
--have within them--
the same values.

00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:21.750
They really are just shifting
and weighing them differently

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:22.630
at different times.

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:25.560
And that creates more of
an opportunity for people,

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:26.700
at least, to see the other.

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:28.290
They may not agree.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:30.040
But they begin to see the other.

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:34.870
And that's, in our society,
where so many groups of people

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:37.170
have been so dehumanized,
our first step

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:41.035
is to let people be seen.

00:24:41.035 --> 00:24:42.910
SARAH HANSEN: Through
all the social upheaval

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:45.460
and the many defining
events of 2020,

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:48.460
Professor McDowell has continued
to spend a lot of his time

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:50.720
engaging with students.

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:53.280
He often works with
smaller class sizes.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:55.370
So that he can develop
the close relationships

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.400
he feels are essential.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:24:59.140
This kind of close
contact allows

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:01.960
him to see into students'
lives beyond the classroom.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:04.570
And he shared with me some
insights into what he's seeing

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:07.380
and what educators
can do to help.

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:11.300
CEASAR MCDOWELL: I think
students are dealing with a lot

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:11.840
right now.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:13.570
And it's not just
because of COVID.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:17.143
I think our society is
in a huge transition.

00:25:17.143 --> 00:25:18.560
I can't tell you
how many students

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:25.860
I have right now who don't feel
connected to their families

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:28.710
anymore because of what's
going on politically

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:32.350
in this country, that disruption
that people are sitting with.

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:36.370
And so then you ask,
how do we, as educators,

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:38.170
knowing they're
sitting with this,

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:42.400
help them to
actually move forward

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:44.590
in a way that is
going to allow them

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:48.580
to be generative with other
people in the communities they

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:51.580
work with and also
find their own path?

00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:57.710
And so for me, teaching always
has to be grounded in reality.

00:25:57.710 --> 00:26:00.500
You have to be working with
others who are actually

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:02.660
dealing with the
problem, or the issue,

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:04.712
or who want to do
something, and you just

00:26:04.712 --> 00:26:05.670
have to work with them.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:10.480
And it really is about lifting
up and supporting people

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:13.430
through that process
of working with others

00:26:13.430 --> 00:26:15.860
and working with yourself.

00:26:15.860 --> 00:26:17.390
In some ways, a
lot of my teaching

00:26:17.390 --> 00:26:20.600
is, yes, it's about
substantive issues.

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:24.740
But it is also about helping
people develop, not only

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:28.250
just a theory or practice,
but a reflective strategy

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:30.800
for themselves as they
move through their careers

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:33.735
and through their lives.

00:26:33.735 --> 00:26:36.110
SARAH HANSEN: There's a lot
of excitement about Professor

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:37.890
McDowell's work and teaching.

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:40.070
So we had some really
wonderful listener questions

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:41.660
sent in for this episode.

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:45.050
And we're excited to share
two of them with you now.

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:47.030
Here's one a listener sent in.

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:49.610
For people who are used to
having privileged access

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:52.790
to policy discussion and
implementation spaces,

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:55.100
what habits should
we watch out for?

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:58.160
Are there subconscious
attitudes or behaviors

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:02.344
that get in the way of including
historically excluded people?

00:27:02.344 --> 00:27:05.100
CEASAR MCDOWELL: That's a
really beautiful question.

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:07.600
I think the number
one habit we have

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:10.050
to watch out for is a
habit that we actually

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:13.060
understand the issue.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:15.840
That we fully understand it.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:20.150
We have to make space
for people to be

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:23.240
able to reframe the
issues with understanding.

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:28.170
If we hold onto our
frame, from that position,

00:27:28.170 --> 00:27:31.810
we will never really fully
be able to include others.

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:35.520
So for me, that's
the critical issue.

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:39.220
And that means we have to
make time and space for that.

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:41.520
And that pushes
against so much of what

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:43.852
we who are in these
these policy areas

00:27:43.852 --> 00:27:45.810
think about is because
we try to do everything.

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:46.935
We've got to do everything.

00:27:46.935 --> 00:27:49.360
We got to do it, and
we've got to get it right.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:54.700
And I think the mantra should
be we're going to do something.

00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:56.700
We're not going to
get it quite right.

00:27:56.700 --> 00:27:59.190
You can take some time
to maybe understand

00:27:59.190 --> 00:28:00.395
it a little different.

00:28:00.395 --> 00:28:01.770
We're going to do
something then.

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:03.180
We're not going to
get it quite right.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:05.500
We're going to take time to
understand it differently.

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:08.640
It's like-- because
that's the reality.

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:10.260
It is the reality.

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:13.460
So we have to acknowledge it.

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:16.520
SARAH HANSEN: And to close,
one more listener question,

00:28:16.520 --> 00:28:20.170
one that very well may have been
on your mind while listening.

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:23.230
Is it easier or harder to
have difficult conversations

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:26.530
in small groups or large
town hall settings?

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:28.840
How do we scale up dialogues?

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:32.200
How do you adjust the
questions, the tone, the pacing

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:35.770
to accommodate large
and diverse audiences?

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:37.270
CEASAR MCDOWELL:
I'm a firm believer

00:28:37.270 --> 00:28:42.790
in small scale dialogues to deal
with really difficult issues.

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:46.780
If we try to do it in a large
scale audience, we, one,

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:48.640
you can't have everyone's
voice in there,

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:52.232
which means you're inevitably
silencing certain people.

00:28:52.232 --> 00:28:54.190
And I'm not saying don't
do large-scale events,

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:56.380
because it's good sometimes
just to sit in a place

00:28:56.380 --> 00:28:59.020
and listen, and hear,
and watch others.

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:01.390
That has value.

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:03.550
But the transformational
piece [? everyone is, ?] I

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:08.260
think is being in small
space dialogues with others.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:11.860
I think we need work at
turning our technologies

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:16.090
and designing our communication
systems to support that kind

00:29:16.090 --> 00:29:17.860
of listening and conversation.

00:29:17.860 --> 00:29:19.390
And that's not what we have.

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:21.218
They're driven by other things.

00:29:21.218 --> 00:29:22.760
That's one of the
problems with them.

00:29:22.760 --> 00:29:25.960
So I think we can
get there if we

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:29.600
shift our intention, our
design intention, to that.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:34.090
And the reality of it
is all of our incentives

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:37.566
for creating things like that
are in the opposite direction.

00:29:37.566 --> 00:29:38.710
[LAUGHS]

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:41.177
It's like, it's really
hard to build technologies

00:29:41.177 --> 00:29:42.760
that are designed
for the margins that

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:44.650
are going to actually scale.

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:47.860
Because you have all these
other things going on.

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:49.100
It's a challenge.

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.160
I don't think it's a
challenge that can't be met.

00:29:55.705 --> 00:29:57.830
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
